First, spear and non-spear phishing emails are quite different in forwarding relationships. For spear phishing emails, they are speciﬁc to individual or corporate executives, so they are unlikely to be sent or forwarded on a large scale. On the contrary, for non-spear phishing emails, they are more likely to be sent or forwarded for multiple times. In order to take full advantage of this big difference, we get the forwarding relationships of each email.
In addition to forwarding features, spear and non-spear phishing emails may all contain URL, but the number and frequency of visits are quite different. Nowadays, there are some websites specializing in collecting the reputation of Internet Protocol Address(IP) and URL, such as [8], [9], these reputation information has high practical value because it is judged and uploaded by experts or safety scanning engine. Links appeared in emails are very likely to be recorded, so we can quickly obtain related reputation information from these websites. In our work, we bring VT [8] and PT [9] to gain reputation features, including IP, URL, Domain, etc.
Second, in security ﬁelds, there exists one huge challenge that the number of malicious samples are much smaller than the number of normal samples, and in our work, this problem is even more prominent. In order to reduce the impact of unbalanced data, we utilize a data enhancement algorithm SMOTE [10] based on K-means clustering named KM-SMOTE to expand the dataset.
In this paper, we focus on distinguishing spear phishing emails from non-spear phishing emails and propose an effective machine learning method. Speciﬁcally, to fully explore the features of spear phishing emails, we extracted a total of 27 features from emails and third-party threat intelligence platforms, then utilized KM-SMOTE algorithm to reduce the impact of unbalanced data, ﬁnally we applied 4 machine learning algorithms to identify spear phishing emails. To the best of our knowledge, this is the ﬁrst work combining with threat intelligence platforms for identifying spear phishing emails. The signiﬁcant contributions of this paper are as follows:
• Our proposed approach does not require any historical email records, or other auxiliary logs, etc
