II. RELATED WORK
A. Phishing emails detection
Over the past decades, there were many research on phishing emails detection, most of them applied machine learning, deep learning or NLP methods to solve the problem. Mayank Pandey [13] presented text and data mining in tandem to detect the phishing emails, they applied Multilayer Perceptron, Decision Trees, SVM and Logistic Regression(LR) for classiﬁcation. Fergus [14] used individual techniques and achieved 97.15% of accuracy. Maher [15] proposed a model to detect phishing websites, they used fuzzy data mining and got 83.7% of accuracy rate.
In addition to machine learning, there were some work using emails’ structural and semantic features. Madhusudhanan [16] used the inherent structural characteristics that are commonly presented in emails to detect phishing emails. Bergholz [17] introduced features generated by adaptively trained Dynamic Markov Chains and novel latent Class-Topic Models, the result showed that their work reduced the number of misclassiﬁed emails to 1/3 compared to previous work. Rakesh Verma [18] presented a general semantic feature selection method for text problems based on the statistical t-test and WordNet, they got 95% of accuracy on detecting phishing emails with 2.24% of false positive rate.
B. Spear phishing emails detection technology
Spear phishing emails often use identity hiding and impersonation attacks, but everyone has it’s own writing habits, so authorship-based identiﬁcation are widely used for detecting spear phishing emails. Mahmoud Khonji [1] presented a new framework to mitigate spear phishing attacks via the use of document authorship techniques called ASCAI, ASCAI informs the user of possible mismatches between the writing styles of a received email and of trusted authors by studying the email’s body itself. Gianluca Stringhini [20] proposed a detection model named IDENTITYMAILER, which validates the authorship of emails by learning the typical emails-sending behavior of users over time, one year later, Gianluca Stringhini

[2] reﬁned and optimized their IDENTITYMAILER model, they added the Composition and sending habits features, the result achieved 96% of accuracy based on 8000 history emails. Duman, Sevtap [3] presented an automated model named emails Proﬁler, they extracted a total of 222 features, then subsequent emails are compared to these models to detect characteristic indicators of spear phishing attacks, the result showed that their model got 98% of accuracy.
Apart from authorship-based methods, there were some other ways combining with big data or the reputation information to detect spear phishing emails. Prateek Dewan [6] made a trial that they extracted 9 social features from LinkedIn, and 18 Stylometric features from emails, but the result showed that the features extracted from LinkedIn didn’t help in identifying spear phishing emails. Han, YuFei [21] focused on spear phishing campaign detection, they introduced an afﬁnity graph based semi-supervised learning model for campaign attribution and detection. Grant Ho [5] presented an approach for detecting credential spear phishing attacks in enterprise settings, they only missed two spear phishing emails in over 370 million real-world emails with less than 0.005% of false positive rate.
III. DATASET COLLECTION METHODOLOGY
A. Email dataset
Our dataset consists of a combination of spear and nonspear phishing emails. We got 417 spear phishing emails and 13916 non-spear phishing emails including benign and phishing emails from the companies we cooperated with. These spear phishing emails were manually discovered and conﬁrmed by security experts.
Our dataset are all formatted email logs, which contain 12 ﬁelds extracted from raw network trafﬁc data, including IP, Subject, Body and Attachment, time, etc. For security reasons, all sensitive information, including senders and receivers, has been obfuscated in all subsequent analyses.
B. Data description
We obtained some statistical information on the dataset. For non-spear phishing emails, there are 5562 benign emails and 8404 common phishing emails. Fig. 1 (a) shows the average number of URL, domain, attachment and forwarding in each email. We found that the average number of URL, attachment and forwarding in benign emails was the highest. spear and phishing emails have similar number of URL, attachment and forwarding, but they have big difference in domain. Further observations revealed that in phishing emails, there are multiple URLs corresponding to the same domain, but in most spear emails, there are exactly one URL in emails.
Fig. 1 (b) presents the percentage of emails containing URL, domain, attachment and forwarding. It clearly shows that the percentage of spear phishing emails containing URL and domain are far more than phishing and benign emails, which is interesting compared to Fig. 1 (a). Although there are only about 20% of phishing and benign emails containing URL, the average number of URL is higher than spear ones, which

