A. Feature Extraction
We extracted a set of 21 Stylometric features from emails, containing subject features, body features and attachment features, 3 forwarding features from EFRGD, and 3 reputation features from VT and PT. Features are described in Table I.

Features marked with ”∗” (see Table I) have been previously used by researchers to classify spam and phishing emails [6], [23], and features marked with ”+” is ﬁrst proposed by us. Fig. 4 shows the process of creating our ﬁnal feature vector.
B. Forwarding features
Forwarding features characterized the forwarding situation of email. For spear phishing emails, it has speciﬁc target recipient, and for common phishing emails, it may delivered to manty unrelated people, intuitively if one email have been forwarded many times, and the recipients have no relationship, it is very unlikely to be spear phishing email. So one of our assumptions is that the forwarding of the spear phishing email is small and the recipients are all related.
Forwarding features including the forwarding scale Forwarding Num, the relationship between the sender and the receiver Forwarding SRsame, and the relationship between the recipients Forwarding RSame. We designed EFRGD to store the forwarding relationship of each email.
